\section*{Partition and mean-length exponents for macroscopic triangular honeycomb chords}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Critical macroscopic triangle chords with both ports summed are claimed to have partition order $R^{3/4}$ and mean-length exponent $4/3$; two vacuum caps select a cylinder amplitude of exponent $1/6$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Transfer fusion and positive boundary mass build a normalized disk vacuum. A residue formula with initially independent twists identifies distinct-site cap moments before common-twist specialization; this amplitude comparison is then transferred to triangle chord sums.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Critical macroscopic triangle chords with both ports summed are claimed to have partition order $R^{3/4}$ and mean-length exponent $4/3$; two vacuum caps select a cylinder amplitude of exponent $1/6$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 237, the selected Lean statements give critical-strip mass and displacement exponents, bridge summability, and existence of pulled free energy. The $4/3$ bridge-length and $3/4$ spatial laws and the other polygon/chord exponents are outside these statements. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

